9 Models
********

Click the model in the header line, or run ‘M-x harmless-pick-model’.
Harmless asks for a logged-in connection, then a model, then a reasoning
effort.  A provider with one model skips the model prompt.  A model that
does not take an effort skips the effort prompt.

   You can also set the pieces separately.  ‘M-x harmless-set-model’
changes the model.  ‘M-x harmless-set-reasoning-effort’ changes the
effort.  An empty effort clears the session's own value and falls back
to ‘harmless-default-reasoning-effort’.

   Models whose names start with ‘grok-4’, ‘gpt-5’, or ‘gpt-6’, and
Claude models, accept an effort.  The usual values are ‘low’, ‘medium’,
‘high’, and ‘xhigh’.  Claude also accepts ‘max’.  A model id such as
‘grok-4.6-xhigh’ sets both the model and the effort.

   Nil for the default effort omits the parameter.  For grok-4.6, the
provider then uses ‘high’.

   The choice is saved with the session.  The next turn uses it.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Tools,  Next: Instructions,  Prev: Models,  Up: Top

10 Tools
********

The model does not change your files by printing a diff.  It calls
tools.  You see each call in the transcript.  Permission rules are in
*note Permissions::.

   Paths are relative to the project root unless you pass an absolute
path that is still inside that root.  A path that escapes the project is
refused.  An empty path is refused.  The filesystem root is not a
project: a search there is refused, and Harmless will not store its own
state there.

   A detached chat is offered ‘memory_read’, ‘memory_remember’,
‘memory_write_topic’, and ‘ask_user’.  *Note Memory::.  *Note
Questions::.

* Menu:

* File tools::  Read, list, write, and replace.
* Search::      Find files and lines.
* Shell::       Run a command.
* Questions::   Ask one question with choices.


File: harmless.info,  Node: File tools,  Next: Search,  Up: Tools

10.1 File tools
===============

‘read_file’
     Read a project file.  ‘offset’ is the first line, counting from 1.
     ‘limit’ is how many lines to return.  The result is cut at
     ‘harmless-read-file-max-bytes’ (100000).  A cut result ends with
     ‘[truncated]’.

‘list_dir’
     List the names in a directory.  A directory name ends with a slash.
     The default path is the project root.

‘write_file’
     Create or replace a file with the given contents.  This is an edit.

‘replace’
     Replace one unique string in a file.  If the string occurs more
     than once, the call fails unless ‘replace_all’ is true.  This is an
     edit.

     In plan mode, ‘write_file’ and ‘replace’ fail.  The model must
     write ‘plan.md’ with ‘write_plan’.  *Note Plan mode::.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Search,  Next: Shell,  Prev: File tools,  Up: Tools

10.2 Search
===========

‘glob’
     Find files whose names match a glob.  The pattern may be a basename
     or a relative path.

‘grep’
     Search file contents for a regular expression.  The optional
     arguments are ‘path’, ‘glob’, ‘engine’, ‘context’, ‘type’, and
     ‘multiline’.

   ‘path’ is a directory inside the project.  A trailing slash is the
same directory.  ‘glob’ limits basenames, for example ‘*.el’.

   ‘engine’ is ‘rg’, ‘ag’, or ‘elisp’.  With no engine, Harmless uses
ripgrep if ‘rg’ is on ‘exec-path’, then ag if ‘ag’ is on ‘exec-path’,
then an Emacs Lisp scan.

   Ripgrep and ag skip ignored files and the ‘.git’ directory.  They do
search hidden files, so a file such as ‘.harmless’ is visible.  The
Emacs Lisp scan reads every regular file under the directory, including
ignored files and ‘.git’.

   ‘context’ is the number of lines to show around each hit.  A hit is
printed as ‘path:line:text’.  A context line is printed as
‘path-line-text’.

   ‘type’ is a file type that the chosen engine knows.  Ripgrep types
come from ‘rg --type-list’.  Ag types come from ‘ag --list-file-types’.
The Emacs Lisp scan has no file types.

   ‘multiline’ lets the pattern cross lines.  Only ripgrep can do that.
Ag refuses it.  The Emacs Lisp scan refuses it.

   The pattern is required, and it is case-sensitive.  An ag pattern
cannot start with a hyphen.  A long result is cut at the same byte cap
as ‘read_file’.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Shell,  Next: Questions,  Prev: Search,  Up: Tools

10.3 Shell
==========

‘run_shell’ runs one command in the project root through your shell.
Each command starts there.  A ‘cd’ in one command does not change the
next command.

   The command is killed after ‘harmless-shell-timeout-seconds’ (120).
The text returned to the model, and shown in the transcript, is cut at
‘harmless-shell-output-max-bytes’ (20000).  A timeout is an error,
followed by whatever output was captured.

   Plan mode does not inspect the command.  A shell command can still
write files while a plan is being written.  *Note Plan mode::.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Questions,  Prev: Shell,  Up: Tools

10.4 Questions
==============

‘ask_user’ asks one question.  ‘question’ is the prompt.  ‘choices’ are
the answers.  Emacs asks with completion, and the answer must be one of
those choices.  The tool returns ‘The user chose: ’ followed by that
answer.

   A missing or blank question is refused.  A missing choice list, an
empty list, a blank choice, a choice that spans lines, and a repeated
choice are refused.  A choice is text.  A value such as ‘/’ or ‘lisp/’
is returned as that text.  Cancelling the prompt returns ‘The user
cancelled the question.’

   The tool does not ask for permission first.  A detached chat can use
it.  Plan mode can use it.


