11 Instructions
***************

Harmless reads these names in each directory, from your home directory
down to the session directory:

  1. ‘CLAUDE.md’, when ‘harmless-claude-md’ says to read it

  2. ‘AGENTS.md’

  3. ‘HARMLESS.md’

  4. ‘.harmless/HARMLESS.md’

   ‘harmless-claude-md’ has three values.  ‘nil’ never reads
‘CLAUDE.md’.  ‘t’ always reads it.  ‘anthropic’, the default, reads it
only when the session model id starts with ‘claude-’.  A model such as
‘grok-4.6’ does not match.  ‘CLAUDE.md’ is read before the other three
names, so those later files win when they disagree.

   A file applies to its directory and everything under it.  Outer
directories come first.  At one directory, the files are read in the
order above.  When two files disagree, the later one wins.  Empty files
are skipped.  The walk stops at your home directory and at the
filesystem root.

   An instruction file can include another file by writing ‘@path’.  The
path is relative to the file that contains the import, not the session
directory.  A backslash before a space is part of the path.  The file's
text replaces the import.  An import inside inline code or a fenced code
block is left as written.  A quoted path is left as written.  The file
must stay inside the session directory.  Harmless refuses an empty path,
a missing file, a directory, a cycle, and a path that leaves the
session.  The filesystem root is not a directory an import may enter.

   The text is sent with every turn.  It is not copied into the
transcript.  A detached chat does not walk ‘default-directory’ looking
for these files.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Skills,  Next: Memory,  Prev: Instructions,  Up: Top

12 Skills
*********

A skill is a directory that contains ‘SKILL.md’.  The file starts with
‘name’ and ‘description’ in YAML frontmatter.  If the name is missing,
Harmless uses the directory name.  If the description is missing, it
uses the first paragraph of the body.

   Each turn receives a catalog: the name, the description, and the
path.  The body is not sent until the model reads that file with
‘read_file’.

   Harmless looks for a ‘skills’ directory inside these vendor
directories, from your home directory down to the session:

‘.agents/skills’
‘.codex/skills’
     Codex.

‘.claude/skills’
     Claude Code.

‘.grok/skills’
     Grok Build.

‘.harmless/skills’
     Harmless.

   In one directory, a later entry in that list replaces an earlier
skill of the same name.  A skill closer to the session replaces one of
the same name farther away.  ‘/etc/codex/skills’ is included when it
exists, at the lowest priority.  Skills that ship inside the Codex
application, and are not files on disk, are not read.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Memory,  Next: Plan mode,  Prev: Skills,  Up: Top

13 Memory
*********

Memory is Markdown under ‘harmless-directory’, in ‘memory’.  Global
notes apply to every project.  Workspace notes belong to one project.  A
detached chat has workspace memory unavailable and global memory
available.

   Each scope has three directories:

‘topics’
     Curated notes, one file per topic.

‘observations/_inbox’
     New facts, not yet folded into a topic.

‘archive’
     Inbox files that ‘harmless-dream’ has folded.

   ‘MEMORY.md’ in each scope is a generated index.  A turn receives the
index, not the notes.

‘M-x harmless-remember’
     Save a title and a note in workspace memory.  A prefix argument
     saves it in global memory.

‘M-x harmless-dream’
     Fold the inbox into topics for this project and for global memory.
     The inbox files move to ‘archive’.

   The model uses three tools.

‘memory_read’
     Read one memory file.  ‘scope’ is ‘workspace’ or ‘global’.

‘memory_remember’
     Append a fact to the inbox.  ‘topic’ is an optional slug, such as
     ‘emacs’.

‘memory_write_topic’
     Replace one topic file with the full Markdown you supply.


